<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hill of Uisneach</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Uisnech, Uisneach</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hill of Uisneach is a low hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-westmeath">County Westmeath</a>, traditionally regarded as the sacred centre of Ireland, where the ancient provinces met. Excavations have found ritual and royal structures from prehistory into the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and it was associated with the festival of Bealtaine and with the kings of Mide. The Catstone on its slope was held to mark the centre of Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hill-of-uisneach.jpg" alt="Hill of Uisneach" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abi Skipp · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hill_of_Uisneach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
